STRIDE & BASE | Issue 223 | March 6, 2026

THIS WEEK: Base phase, without the boredom

Somewhere around week ten of an easy-only block, most runners hit the same wa=
ll: not fatigue but tedium. The data says keep going, the brain says do somet=
hing interesting. A few ways to keep the aerobic work aerobic and still give =
the brain something to chew on:

1. Change the surface, not the effort. Trails, grass and gravel give your fee=
t and ankles new work at the same heart rate.
2. Strides are free. Six 20-second strides at the end of two easy runs a week=
 keep leg speed alive without a measurable cost to recovery. Former track run=
ners in particular lose their turnover faster than their aerobic engine.
3. Cross-train with intent. A 90-minute ride at Zone 2 is not a punishment fo=
r an injury. It's the same aerobic stimulus with zero impact, and it's how so=
me of the highest-volume runners we know get to 12 hours a week without break=
ing.
4. Track something besides pace. Aerobic decoupling, cadence drift, how many =
times you checked your watch. Pick one and watch it improve.

READER MAILBAG
"My heart rate goes from 140 to 158 on the same loop in March humidity versus=
 February cold." That's normal. Use the cap, slow down, and remember the adap=
tation doesn't care what your pace looked like.

UPCOMING
Our spring half-marathon guide lands March 20, with 12-week plans for 1:45, 1=
:30 and sub-1:25.

The Stride & Base editors

